BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Honorlock Practice and AI Agreement Quiz (Proctored) [IHUM 201-022
 ]
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260912
DTSTAMP:20260903T182900Z
UID:event-assignment-1466255
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:--------------------------------------------------------------
 --\nOrientation Honorlock Practice and AI Agreement Quiz (Proctored)\n----
 ------------------------------------------------------------\n\nIn this ac
 tivity\, you will review the course AI policy and set up Honorlock for pro
 ctored exams. You will then complete a short practice quiz that includes a
  reflection on the AI policy.\n\nPurpose of the Quiz\n-------------------
 \n\nThis quiz serves two purposes: to help you become familiar with the on
 line proctoring process and to confirm your understanding of the course AI
  policy.\n\nBefore continuing in the course\, review the AI policy below\,
  complete the Honorlock setup\, and submit the quiz. Doing this now will h
 elp you prepare for your first proctored exam and avoid technical problems
  later.\n\nThe quiz includes a short reflection on the AI policy. Your res
 ponse will also provide an example of your natural writing style\, which m
 ay be helpful if an AI detector produces a false positive in the future. W
 rite in the same voice and style you would normally use for a paper in thi
 s course.\n\nRead the AI Policy\n------------------\n\nArtificial Intellig
 ence can be useful in a host of situations\, but not in any assignments 
 and papers for this course unless explicitly stated. I know other courses 
 may encourage you to use AI\, but remember\, your HUMANities class needs
  to be 100% HUMAN.\n\nDevelop Critical Thinking Skills\n-----------------
 ---------------\n\nThe purpose of IHUM 201 is to develop critical thinking
  skills as you work your way through ideas in your own head\, gain new ins
 ights as you encounter new ideas\, discuss what you're learning about with
  others\, and come to your own conclusions. You need to write about those 
 conclusions in your own voice. Do not be tempted to delegate your thinkin
 g or writing to AI. ChatGPT\, Gemini\, Grammarly\, CoPilot\, and the grow
 ing list cannot be used to write any part of your assignments and papers. 
 Not only is it academically dishonest to pass off AI-generated ideas and t
 ext as your own\, but the AI bot that generates that text creates a pretty
  soulless product. It will simplify your complex ideas in the name of clar
 ity and tone.\n\nBlessed with a Unique Mind and Voice\n-------------------
 -----------------\n\nYou—yes\, human you—are blessed with a unique\, i
 ndividual mind and a unique\, individual voice. Believe in your ability to
  use both. I genuinely want to hear what human you has to say. If you are 
 worried about your grammar\, spelling\, organization\, etc\, DO NOT run yo
 ur writing through AI. DO NOT ask any AI to write your outline or build yo
 ur thesis. Talk with a friend\, family member\, or classmate. Visit the [
 BYU Writing and Research CenterLinks to an external site.] (https://rwc.by
 u.edu/) either in person or virtually. Set up a meeting with your TA to g
 o over your writing. Read your writing out loud to hear what it actually s
 ounds like. Your skills will improve with your effort\, but they will not 
 improve if AI does these things for you. \n\nFor all writing in this clas
 s\, you may use on the basic spell and grammar check in Microsoft Word\, G
 oogle Docs\, or in your web browser. TURN OFF Grammarly plugins or any s
 imilar extensions\, which will most likely result in your work being flagg
 ed for AI use. \n\nIf English is your second language\, again\, DO NOT ru
 n your work through AI checkers. To be accepted at BYU\, you had to show
  a level of English proficiency through exams such as the TOEFL. Your En
 glish language skills will only improve through practice\, not through AI
  "fixing" your writing. Please utilize the resources mentioned above\, an
 d\, if you feel it's necessary\, reach out to your TA to let them know tha
 t English is not your primary language. We will take that into account whe
 n grading your assignments and papers and give helpful feedback to improve
  your language skills. \n\nIf an assignment or paper is flagged for cont
 aining any AI-generated text\, that assignment or paper will receive a zer
 o. Your TA or I will reach out to in an email to let you know that your wo
 rk has been flagged so we can talk about why it was flagged and whether or
  not you adhered to the above AI policy. Submitting a second assignment or
  paper that gets flagged for AI use is cause for dismissal from the class
 \, a failing grade\, and the need for a meeting with the Honor Code Office
  to learn more about academic honesty.  \n\nIf you cannot commit to not 
 using AI in all assignments (unless explicitly stated)\, this course isn't
  for you! \n\nThis AI policy is based on the BYU Comparative Arts and Let
 ters Position on AI. We recommend you familiarize yourself with it as well
 . \n\nBYU Comparative Arts and Letters Position on AI\n------------------
 -----------------------------\n\nPractice\n--------\n\nAI and LLMs in the
   BYU Comparative Arts & Letters Classroom\n\nYou won’t find the same A
 I policies in a humanities class as what you may encounter in other fields
  across campus. Reading\, writing\, brainstorming\, editing\, visualizing
 \, creating\, and analyzing texts and artworks are crucial enterprises tha
 t should not be offloaded without clear-eyed deliberation about their effe
 cts (in all realms - cognitive\, ethical\, social\, aesthetic\, spiritual
 \, etc.). Pay attention to each of your instructor’s policies and be rea
 dy to commit to humanistic work in its richness and rigor. As a peculiar p
 eople\, we should not be afraid to go against the cultural grain--to go sl
 ower when the world demands we speed up\; to savor instead of consume\; to
  question\, rather than prompt\; to listen more deeply rather than scroll 
 more voraciously\; to painstakingly craft rather than reproduce or simulat
 e. We understand the inseparable relationships of culture and technology\,
  materiality and media\, and believe that ongoing experience and spiritual
  discernment are vital to navigating those relationships in our teaching\,
  research\, and spiritual mission. As Latter-day Saint humanists we believ
 e education is aimed at nothing less than building the kingdom for the glo
 ry of God\, whose glory is not artificial\, but authentic and embodied lov
 ing intelligence.  \n\n \n\nPrinciples\n----------\n\nOn the importance
  of being human(ists)\n\nOur understanding of the humanities as Latter-day
  Saints recognizes embodied experience as the central purpose of our morta
 l journey. The humanities are an exploration of mortal experience: of the 
 historical and cultural forces that shape us and confront us as human bein
 gs\, and which provoke innumerable forms of human expressions and response
 s over time and across space. These expressions and responses in their var
 ied forms are the humanist’s objects of study. In the humanities\, form
 \, content\, and context are interconnected in the process of making meani
 ng\, in contrast to other disciplines which may regard objects primarily i
 n terms of the content they convey or their perceived utility. In addition
 \, the kinds of cognitive offloading and deskilling that these technologie
 s appear to foster run counter to many of the goals of a BYU education and
  to our shared mortal purpose of gaining experience and intelligence throu
 gh lived experience. \n\nOn “generative” technologies\n\nTechnologies
  that purport to generate language at unprecedented scale and speed obscur
 e the practices that backstop those technologies (some of which are ethica
 lly and morally dubious)\; they also make the practice of writing (and wri
 ting-as-thinking) seem trivial and automatable. All of this is of great co
 ncern to us as Latter-Day Saint humanists. We view large language models (
 chatbots\, LLMs\, “AI”)  not as neutral or transparent tools of infor
 mation\, but products of cultural and corporate ideologies that must be th
 e objects of study rather than means of outsourcing creativity and analysi
 s. Any use of AI in the humanities is necessarily an exercise in critical 
 thinking\, not simply a means of transmission.\n\nAcademic Integrity\n----
 --------------\n\nPlease carefully review the following information regard
 ing academic integrity.\n\nBy accessing BYU Online academic exams\, you ac
 knowledge that you have read and understand the BYU Academic Honesty Polic
 y. For clarity\, we want to remind you that the BYU Academic Honesty Polic
 y and all related policies apply to you when you take this test or quiz\, 
 and we want to check your recognition of this fact. You agree to uphold th
 e principles of academic honesty and integrity\, follow all exam rules and
  guidelines (including the use of proctoring services)\, and adhere to the
  consequences of any violations as outlined in the BYU Academic Honesty Po
 licy. Please note that you must agree to each requirement in this quiz to 
 proceed with this course\n\nHonorlock\n---------\n\n[Preparing for Your Ex
 am] (https://byu.instructure.com/courses/10881/pages/exam-instructions?mod
 ule_item_id=2232810)\n\nModule Item Completion (browser only)
