DESCRIPTION:------------------------------------------------\nLesson 2 The
  Iliad Project (Part 2) - Worksheet\n-------------------------------------
 -----------\n\nIn this second part of the project\, you'll document some o
 f the epithets and attributions you found. Then you will write two thought
 ful paragraphs: one explaining which hero you think best represents areté
 \, and another exploring the Greek cultural ideals shown in the story. All
  writing must be your own ideas and thoughts. Do not ask any AI to come up
  with outlines or ideas for this project. Do not run your writing through 
 any AI checkers including Grammarly\, Copilot\, or any other LLMs. You can
  use the basic spell-check and grammar-check on Microsoft Word or Google D
 ocs. Evidence of any AI-generated text will result in a zero grade. \n\nF
 ill Out Worksheet\n------------------\n\nPlease use the [Iliad Worksheet] 
 (https://byu.instructure.com/courses/36390/files/13583144?wrap=1)[Download
  Iliad Worksheet] ($CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/file_ref/mastercourse_7407_97
 8_aff0d4f351ade5ee587c6b24f6157d78/download?download_frd=1) to complete t
 he following steps. \n\nStep 1: List Favorite Epithets \n---------------
 ----------------\n\nUsing your personal reading and analysis\, list in the
  worksheet:\n\n* Achilles: Three of his most important\, interesting\, or 
 revealing epithets (not the first three\, but the best three)\n\n* Hector:
  Three of his most important\, interesting\, or revealing epithets (not th
 e first three\, but the best three)\n\n* Other characters: Three epithets 
 you liked for any other characters (not the first three\, but the best thr
 ee)\n\nInclude the book and line number for each epithet. For example\, 1.
 30–32 means Book 1\, lines 30–32.\n\nYou may write down epithets you f
 ind important as you read and then narrow it down to the top three for eac
 h\, or you may return to your highlighted and annotated text after reading
 . Decide which way works best for you. \n\nStep 2: List Defining Attribut
 es\n--------------------------------\n\nWe are now narrowing our focus to 
 just two characters: Hector and Achilles.\n\nList of three attributes in t
 he worksheet that define Hector and three that define Achilles. These shou
 ld be based on the characters’ words or actions\, not just their epithet
 s. How do they behave? What do they say that helps you understand their ch
 aracter?\n\nFor each bullet point:\n\n* State the attribute as you interpr
 et it.\n\n* Give supporting evidence from the passage.\n\n* Include the bo
 ok and line numbers.\n\nUse this format:\n\nAttribute: Evidence from the t
 ext (book.line-line)\n\nExample:\n\nLoving father: He kisses his “dear s
 on” and holds him in his arms (6.469-470)\n\nStep 3: Argue for Areté—
 Hector or Achilles?\n-------------------------------------------\n\nWrite 
 one thoughtful paragraph in the worksheet (at least five sentences long).
 \n\nArgue whether Hector or Achilles is a better example of the Greek conc
 ept of areté as discussed in this lesson.\n\n* Begin with a thesis state
 ment that clearly identifies your choice and gives your main reasons.\n\n*
  Use the rest of the paragraph to explain and support your thesis.\n\n* Us
 e your notes about epithets\, actions\, and character qualities as evidenc
 e.\n\n* Include citations from the text (book.line-line).\n\nThesis exampl
 e:\n\nHector is a better example of areté because _____ and _____.\n\nSte
 p 4: Greek Cultural Ideals\n-----------------------------\n\nWrite one tho
 ughtful paragraph in the worksheet (at least five sentences long).\n\nDesc
 ribe at least two important Ancient Greek cultural ideals you noticed whil
 e reading the Iliad.\n\n* Begin with a thesis statement that identifies th
 e ideals you will discuss.\n\n* Explain each ideal in your own words.\n\n*
  Use your notes and specific evidence from the text to support your ideas.
 \n\n* Include citations from the text (book.line-line).\n\nStep 5: Cite th
 e Iliad\n----------------------\n\nTo cite information in your paragraphs
 \, make sure to cite the book and the line numbers\, as you practiced in y
 our list-making. Because I assume you are quoting from the Iliad througho
 ut the paper\, you do not need to keep writing Iliad in your citation af
 ter the first time. You do\, however\, need to cite it in a Works Cited at
  the end of the paper.  If you are unclear on how to do that\, look up u
 sing MLA format from the Purdue [OWL] (https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/resea
 rch_and_citation/mla_style/mla_formatting_and_style_guide/mla_formatting_a
 nd_style_guide.html?_ga=2.43647334.2103559497.1526860800-1100032269.152686
 0800) site.\n\nView the [title page of Powell’s ] (https://research-ebs
 co-com.byu.idm.oclc.org/c/w2m2q6/ebook-viewer/pdf/73do6f5whb/page/pp_v)[Il
 iad] (https://research-ebsco-com.byu.idm.oclc.org/c/w2m2q6/ebook-viewer/pd
 f/73do6f5whb/page/pp_v) so that you can make sure your works cited incl
 udes correct information.  \n\nWhat to Submit\n--------------\n\nYou wi
 ll submit two items on this page\n\n* Your filled-out [Iliad Worksheet] (h
 ttps://byu.instructure.com/courses/36390/files/13583144?wrap=1) as a DOCX
  or PDF file.\n\n* Statement in the submission comment box saying\, "This 
 is all my own work\, and I didn't use AI."\n\n* If this statement is not t
 rue\, please redo this assignment using your own work before you submit it
 .\n\n* If this statement is not included in the submission comment\, the a
 ssignment will not be graded.\n\nNote: you must submit your annotations (P
 art 1) on the previous page before this worksheet (Part 2) will be graded.
 \n\nModule Item Completion (browser only)
