BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Antigone Project (Part 1) - Annotations [IHUM 201-022]
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260923
DTSTAMP:20260903T183000Z
UID:event-assignment-1471349
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:----------------------------------------------------\nLesson 5
  The Antigone Project (Part 1) - Annotations\n----------------------------
 ------------------------\n\nNow that you have a stronger foundation in Gre
 ek tragedy\, you get to read Sophocles’s Antigone. The actual reading ge
 nerally takes between 45-60 minutes.\n\nThe goal of this reading is to app
 ly some of the primary Greek terms you’ve learned in this lesson into pr
 actice. This will solidify your learning and will also help you respond to
  short-answer questions on the Unit 1 Check.\n\nInstructions\n------------
 \n\nDownload a copy of [Antigone] (https://research-ebsco-com.byu.idm.oclc
 .org/c/w2m2q6/ebook-viewer/pdf/ea6hcqojjz/page/pp_xiii) to highlight. You 
 do not need to download the entire book\, just the pages that encompass th
 e play. You can do this by clicking the download button next to "Antigone"
  in the table of contents.\n\n[] (https://byu.instructure.com/courses/3639
 0/files/13605205/preview?hidden=1)\n\nWhat to Highlight\n-----------------
 \n\nUse these colors to highlight your PDF copy of Antigone:\n\n* Yellow: 
 Examples of deios. Where do you see humans being either awe-inspiring or t
 errible (or maybe both at the same time)? It is good to write notes in the
  margins about who is behaving this way.\n\n* Green: Examples of philos. W
 here do you see the characters interacting in terms of philos or friends a
 s described by Dr. Jeppesen.\n\n* Blue: Examples of echthros. Where do you
  see the characters interacting in terms of echthros or enemies within the
 ir own community as described by Dr. Jeppesen.\n\nWhat to Take Notes On\n-
 --------------------\n\nTake notes on the following in the margins of your
  PDF copy of Antigone\n\n* Hamartia: Write notes in the margins where you 
 see a character “missing the mark.”\n\n* Catharsis: On the last page o
 f the reading\, write what you experienced emotionally by reading this pla
 y. Also\, take note of whether or not you think your experience is similar
  to the catharsis the Greeks\, as you understand them from your studies\, 
 would have felt. Does your modern experience make you experience the play 
 differently?\n\n* Modern connections: Use whatever method you like (stars
 \, outlining\, underlining) when you notice a scene that feels like it cou
 ld be translated to a modern setting. The conflict is like a modern confli
 ct. The consequences may be different in our modern world\, but you could 
 see something similar happening today.\n\nThe following are helpful terms 
 to remember\, but you are not required to take notes on them.\n\n* Peripet
 eia: Note in the margins where you see someone undergo a reversal of fortu
 ne. Is it from good to bad or bad to good?\n\n* Anagnorisis: Take note if 
 anyone seems to recognize or realize what has been lost by their mistake o
 r reversal of fortune.\n\nWhat to Submit\n--------------\n\nYou will submi
 t two items on this page\n\n* Your annotated Antigone PDF (highlights and 
 notes in the margins).\n\n* Statement in the submission comment box saying
 \, "This is all my own work\, and I didn't use AI."\n\n* If this statement
  is not true\, please redo this assignment using your own work before you 
 submit it.\n\n* If this statement is not included in the submission commen
 t\, the assignment will not be graded.\n\nRemember\, the annotation must b
 e your own work. If you submit a PDF of someone else's work\, that is plag
 iarism. If you use AI in any way to annotate your text\, that is academic 
 dishonesty. Both will be reported to the Honor Code Office and will result
  in a zero grade on the entire project. \n\nNote: You must submit the PDF
  of your close reading on this page (Part 1) for your modern adaptation (P
 art 2) to be accepted and graded.\n\nModule Item Completion (browser only)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://byu.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _36390&month=09&year=2026#assignment_1471349
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instruct
 ure-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com/account_74070000000000001/attachments/8409020
 /dp_app.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instructure-uploads.s3.a
 mazonaws.com/account_74070000000000001/attachments/8073295/canvas-byuonlin
 e-app.css"><div id="dp-wrapper" class="dp-wrapper" style="background-color
 : #f9f9f9\; color: #000000\; border-radius: 2px\;">\n<header class="dp-hea
 der dp-border-dir-tblr dp-border-style-solid dp-padding-direction-tblr dp-
 margin-direction-tblr" style="border-width: 8px 0px 0px\; padding-top: 0px
 \; padding-bottom: 0px\; margin-bottom: 0px\; border-radius: 2px\; padding
 -left: 7px\; border-color: #005a38\; background-color: #005a38\; color: #f
 fffff\;">\n<h2 id="page-title" class="dp-heading dp-margin-direction-tblr 
 dp-padding-direction-tblr" style="margin-top: 0px\; margin-bottom: 0px\; p
 adding-bottom: 20px\; padding-top: 18px\; padding-left: 5px\; border-color
 : #2b4226\; color: #ffffff\;">\n<span class="dp-header-pre dp-padding-dire
 ction-tblr dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-text-bold dp-border-style-solid dp-
 border-dir-tblr" style="border-width: 0px 2px 0px 0px\; padding-right: 12p
 x\; background-color: #005a38\; color: #ffffff\;"><span class="dp-header-p
 re-1" style="color: #ffffff\;">Lesson 5</span></span> <span class="dp-head
 er-title dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-padding-direction-tblr" style="margin
 -top: 0px\;">The <em>Antigone</em> Project (Part 1) - Annotations</span>\n
 </h2>\n</header>\n<div class="dp-content-block dp-margin-direction-tblr dp
 -padding-direction-tblr dp-border-style-solid dp-border-dir-all dp-shadow-
 b1 lead" style="margin: 50px 70px 40px\; padding: 25px 35px\; background-c
 olor: #ffffff\; color: #000000\; border-width: 3px\; border-color: #005a38
 \;">\n<p class=""><span>Now that you have a stronger foundation in Greek t
 ragedy\, you get to read Sophocles’s Antigone. The actual reading genera
 lly takes between 45-60 minutes.</span></p>\n<p><span>The goal of this rea
 ding is to apply some of the primary Greek terms you’ve learned in this 
 lesson into practice. This will solidify your learning and will also help 
 you respond to short-answer questions on the Unit 1 Check.</span></p>\n</d
 iv>\n<div class="dp-content-block dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-padding-dire
 ction-tblr dp-shadow-b1 dp-border-dir-none" style="margin: 50px 70px 40px
 \; padding: 0px 35px 25px\; background-color: #ffffff\; color: #000000\; b
 order-width: 5px 0px 0px\; border-color: #005a38\;">\n<h3 id="instructor-v
 ideo-forthcoming" class="dp-border-dir-tblr dp-border-style-solid dp-paddi
