 eview?hidden=1" alt="" data-api-endpoint="https://byu.instructure.com/api/
 v1/courses/36390/files/13605205" data-api-returntype="File" loading="lazy"
 ></span></p>\n<h4 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr cp-txt-dp-primary dp-tex
 t-bold" style="margin-top: 40px\; font-size: 22px\; margin-bottom: 10px\; 
 color: #005a38\;">What to Highlight</h4>\n<hr class="dp-hr-solid-light dp-
 margin-direction-tblr" style="border-color: #eeeeee\; color: #c7c9c7\; mar
 gin-top: 8px\; margin-bottom: 15px\;">\n<p>Use these colors to highlight y
 our PDF copy of Antigone:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<span style="background-color: 
 #fbeeb8\;">Yellow:</span> Examples of <strong>deios</strong>. <span>Where 
 do you see humans being either awe-inspiring or terrible (or maybe both at
  the same time)? It is good to write notes in the margins about who is beh
 aving this way.</span>\n</li>\n<li>\n<span style="background-color: #bfedd
 2\;">Green:</span> E<span>xamples of <strong>philos</strong>. Where do you
  see the characters interacting in terms of philos or friends as described
  by Dr. Jeppesen.</span>\n</li>\n<li>\n<span style="background-color: #c2e
 0f4\;">Blue:</span> <span>Examples of <strong>echthros</strong>. Where do 
 you see the characters interacting in terms of echthros or enemies within 
 their own community as described by Dr. Jeppesen.</span>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h
 4 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr cp-txt-dp-primary dp-text-bold" style="m
 argin-top: 40px\; font-size: 22px\; margin-bottom: 10px\; color: #005a38\;
 ">What to Take Notes On</h4>\n<hr class="dp-hr-solid-light dp-margin-direc
 tion-tblr" style="border-color: #eeeeee\; color: #c7c9c7\; margin-top: 8px
 \; margin-bottom: 15px\;">\n<p>Take notes on the following in the margins 
 of your PDF copy of Antigone</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<strong>Hamartia</strong>: W
 rite notes in the margins where you see a character “missing the mark.
 ”</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Catharsis</strong>: On the last page of the readin
 g\, write what you experienced emotionally by reading this play. Also\, ta
 ke note of whether or not you think your experience is similar to the cath
 arsis the Greeks\, as you understand them from your studies\, would have f
 elt. Does your modern experience make you experience the play differently?
 </li>\n<li>\n<strong>Modern connections</strong>: Use whatever method you 
 like (stars\, outlining\, underlining) when you notice a scene that feels 
 like it could be translated to a modern setting. The conflict is like a mo
 dern conflict. The consequences may be different in our modern world\, but
  you could see something similar happening today.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The foll
 owing are helpful terms to remember\, but you are not required to take not
 es on them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<strong>Peripeteia</strong>: Note in the marg
 ins where you see someone undergo a reversal of fortune. Is it from good t
 o bad or bad to good?</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Anagnorisis</strong>: Take note 
 if anyone seems to recognize or realize what has been lost by their mistak
 e or reversal of fortune.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class="dp-content-bloc
 k dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-padding-direction-tblr dp-shadow-b1 dp-borde
 r-dir-none" style="margin: 50px 70px 40px\; padding: 0px 35px 25px\; backg
 round-color: #ffffff\; color: #000000\; border-width: 5px 0px 0px\; border
 -color: #005a38\;">\n<h3 id="instructor-video-forthcoming" class="dp-borde
 r-dir-tblr dp-border-style-solid dp-padding-direction-tblr dp-margin-direc
 tion-tblr" style="border-width: 0px\; padding-left: 22px\; padding-bottom:
  10px\; padding-top: 12px\; border-color: #005a38\; color: #ffffff\; margi
 n: 0px -35px 30px\; font-size: 24px\; background-color: #005a38\;"><strong
 >What to Submit</strong></h3>\n<p>You will submit two items on this page</
