DESCRIPTION:----------------------------------------------------------\nLe
 sson 5 The Antigone Project (Part 2) - Modernize a Scene\n----------------
 ------------------------------------------\n\nFor this activity\, you will
  take one short section of Antigone and recreate it for a modern audience.
  The setting and language may change\, but the central conflict\, characte
 r motivations\, and key choices should remain recognizable.\n\nDifferent M
 odern Adaptations\n----------------------------\n\nAntigone has been rewri
 tten and restaged for many different cultures and countries. The fact that
  so many of these retellings have happened in the last century points to t
 he continued relevance of the play. Watch the following short video where 
 Dr. Jeppesen will explain how Antigone has been retold in situations rangi
 ng from Nazi occupation in France and Apartheid in South Africa to ISIS re
 cruitment after the War on Terror.\n\nTranscript: Forthcoming\n\nInstructi
 ons\n------------\n\n \n\nDo This Yourself: No Generative AI\n\nThis is j
 ust a reminder not to use ChatGPT or another generative AI tool to brainst
 orm\, draft\, rewrite\, or revise this assignment.\n\nAI might create a cl
 ever adaptation\, but your creative choices are the assignment. Deciding 
 what the conflict means\, what motivates the characters\, and how the scen
 e could happen today will help you better understand Antigone. If AI makes
  those decisions for you here\, that learning is lost. You are creative! T
 rust yourself to come up with something unique and interesting!\n\nStep 1:
  Choose It\n-----------------\n\nChoose one short scene or exchange that i
 nterests you. Look for a clear conflict or turning point\; do not adapt th
 e whole play.\n\nBefore you begin\, identify:\n\n* What does each main cha
 racter want\, and why?\n\n* What power or choice does each character have?
 \n\n* What key choice and consequence must remain?\n\n* What modern situat
 ion creates a similar conflict?\n\nNeed help choosing a scene? \n--------
 --------------------\n\nIf you need help choosing a scene\, consider one o
 f the following that fits the assignment requirements. This list is not co
 mprehensive\; you can choose a scene not included here.\n\n* Antigone asks
  Ismene to help bury Polyneices\; Ismene refuses (pgs. 3-8). \n\n* The Gu
 ard reports the burial\; Creon accuses and threatens him (pgs. 15–20).\n
 \n* The Guard brings in Antigone and explains how she was caught (pgs. 21
 –24).\n\n* Creon questions Antigone\; she defends breaking his law (pgs.
  25–29).\n\n* Ismene tries to share Antigone’s guilt\; Antigone reject
 s her claim (pgs. 29–33).\n\n* Haemon tries to reason with Creon\; their
  disagreement becomes a confrontation (pgs. 34–41).\n\n* Antigone faces 
 her punishment and argues with the Chorus and Creon (pgs. 42–48).\n\n* T
 iresias warns Creon\; Creon refuses to listen and attacks his motives (pgs
 . 50–56).\n\n* The Messenger tells Eurydice what happened to Antigone an
 d Haemon (pgs. 59–64).\n\n* Creon returns and learns the full consequenc
 es of his decisions (pgs. 64–68).\n\nStep 2: Move It to the Modern World
 \n-----------------------------------\n\nMove the scene to a specific mode
 rn setting. It might take place in a family\, school\, workplace\, governm
 ent\, community\, or any other setting you want.\n\nYou may update:\n\n* C
 haracter names (though you don't have to)\n\n* Places and occupations\n\n*
  Objects and customs\n\n* Social and cultural references\n\n* Consequence 
 type (but still keep the level of seriousness the same)\n\nPreserve the or
 iginal conflict\, motivations\, power relationships\, and key choices. Kee
 p the characters complex\; do not turn one side into a simple hero or vill
 ain.\n\nNote: Antigone includes death\, suicide\, grief\, family trauma\, 
 and state violence. You do not need to recreate these events explicitly. Y
 ou may keep serious events offstage or non-graphic\, use another serious c
 onsequence\, or choose another passage.\n\nStep 3: Write the Scene\n------
 -----------------\n\nRecreate the scene in clear\, contemporary language w
 hile keeping it in script format. Label each speaker and include brief sta
 ge directions when needed. Greek tragedies often used poetic meters such a
 s [iambic trimeter] (#dpPopup0Content)\, but you do not need to use poetic
  meter. Write natural dialogue—the way you speak or imagine your charact
 ers would speak today.\n\nUse the “roommate test”: If you can hand the
  script to your roommates and they can perform the scene without asking wh
 o says each line or what is happening\, your script is clear and correctly
  formatted.\n\nThree feet of iambic meter\, which consists of an unstresse
 d syllable followed by a stressed syllable.\n\nYour adaptation should:\n\n
 * Have a clear beginning\, middle\, and end.\n\n* Use a specific and consi
 stent modern setting.\n\n* Show the characters’ key choices and conseque
 nces.\n\n* Show what happens instead of simply summarizing it (be in scrip
 t format).\n\nYour adaptation does not need to be the same length as the o
 riginal scene. As long as you capture the above requirements in a thoughtf
 ul way\, any length is acceptable.\n\nBelow is an example of a modernized 
 scene. We’ve put it in a drop-down because you do not need to read this 
 if you don’t want it to influence your own idea. It will give you a poss
 ible way to approach this\, but it is just one possibility. Have fun with 
 this project! Let your creativity flow! Your writing style will be differe
 nt because you have your own unique experiences\, perspectives\, and voice
 .\n\nGreat Example of a Modernized Scene \n------------------------------
 ------\n\nNote: This example models one possible approach. You can still m
 odernize the same scene\, but do not reuse the setting or specific situati
 on in your own adaptation.\n\n* Scene: Lines 1-97\n\n* Modern Location: Hi
 gh School \n\n* Title: “Stick it to the Man”\n\nAfter school. Ismene 
 sits on her bed\, scrolling through her phone. Antigone rushes into the ro
 om.\n\nAntigone: Sis! Can you believe what an idiot that superintendent Cr
 eon is? \n\nIsmene: I really haven’t heard anything since Polyneices an
 d Eteocles went off to college. I miss them so much. I’ve kinda been out
  of it. \n\nAntigone: Yeah\, I noticed. You good? \n\nIsmene: It’s wha
 tever\, just spill it. \n\nAntigone: Okay\, so Polyneices and Eteocles ju
 st broke track and field state records at that track meet\, right? Amazing
 \, cream of the crop\, rising freshman. Great. Creon just put a display ca
 se in the district athletic building\, and get this. He only put up Eteocl
 es! Can you believe that? It’s such baloney! He says it’s cause Polyne
 ices went to Bull Run High. Bull Run High had that cheating scandal\, but 
 they never proved Polyneices did anything. And as if that wasn’t enough
 \, he said whoever puts Polyneices’ posters around\, wears t-shirts\, or
  does anything to support him is gonna get expelled. It’s up to you and 
 me to make sure Polyneices gets the credit he deserves.  \n\nIsmene: I
 ’m not sure that’s the best idea. \n\nAntigone: Listen\, none of this
  back and forth\, okay? Are you in or out?\n\nIsmene: What are you plannin
 g on doing? \n\nAntigone: I’m gonna break into the district building to
 night\, decorate the display for Polyneices\, and trash Creon’s office.
  \n\nIsmene: Stands in alarm. Are you crazy? \n\nAntigone: Polyneices is
  our brother. It’s not fair he doesn’t get a place on that display cas
 e. He deserves it. I’m gonna show Creon who’s boss. \n\nIsmene: But y
 ou’re gonna get in big trouble! \n\nAntigone: I don’t care. Everyone 
 knows it’s the right thing to do. \n\nIsmene: Antigone. I know you’re
  trying to do what’s best for our family\, but the truth is everyone in 
 our small town already knows how sketchy our family’s past is. It’s ju
 st us here now\, and we get somewhat of a fresh start. I don’t think we 
 should be messing around. \n\nAntigone: Grabs her backpack. Fine\, forget
  it. I’ll do it myself. I’m going to make my big brother proud.\n\nIsm
 ene: Listen\, I love Polyneices\, but it’s not worth risking our futures
  and getting expelled.\n\nAntigone: Excuses\, excuses. It’s fine\, like 
 I said\, I’ll do it myself. \n\nIsmene: Antigone\, please don’t do th
 is. \n\nAntigone: Don’t worry about it. I’m not gonna get caught.\n\n
 Ismene: Just don’t tell anyone else about it. I’ll keep it on the down
  low also. \n\nAntigone: Girl\, shout it out to the rooftops for all I ca
 re. Come on\, live a little!\n\nIsmene: You’re such a martyr\, Antigone.
  \n\nAntigone: What can I say? I live for the applause. \n\nIsmene: Only
  if you don’t get caught. \n\nAntigone: Holds up her phone. When I do g
 et caught\, the deed will already be done. I’m posting it. \n\nIsmene: 
 Why even try it if you’re gonna get caught? \n\nAntigone: My mind’s m
 ade up. Full send. Both our brothers deserve the best.\n\nIsmene: Do what 
 you want then. You’ll still be the most popular girl in school.\n\nAntig
 one exits. Ismene watches her leave\, worried.\n\nPoor Example of a Modern
 ized Scene\n----------------------------------\n\n"I chose the scene where
  Creon questions Antigone after she breaks his law. My adaptation takes pl
 ace at a modern high school. Antigone is a student named Anna\, and Creon 
 is the principal\, Mr. Carter.\n\nAnna’s brother had been suspended for 
 getting into a fight. Mr. Carter told everyone that students were not allo
 wed to talk to him or help him with schoolwork because he needed to learn 
 his lesson. Anna thought this was unfair\, so she secretly brought her bro
 ther his homework. Another student saw her and reported her to the princip
 al.\n\nMr. Carter called Anna into his office and asked why she had broken
  the rule. Anna said that family was more important than school rules. Mr.
  Carter became angry because he believed students should always obey the p
 rincipal. Anna refused to apologize\, so he suspended her. She left the of
 fice feeling proud of herself\, while Mr. Carter went back to work."\n\nNo
 te: This is a weak example because it summarizes the encounter instead of 
 showing it\, does not use script format\, makes the conflict too simple\, 
 and gives the characters little depth. The modern consequence also does no
 t carry the same seriousness as the choice in Antigone.\n\nWhat to Submit
 \n--------------\n\nYou will submit two items on this page\n\n* Your moder
 nized scene as a DOCX or PDF file. Your file should include:\n\n* Original
  scene line numbers\n\n* Modern setting location\n\n* Title of your adapta
 tion\n\n* Script of scene\n\n* Statement in the submission comment box say
 ing\, "This is all my own work\, and I didn't use AI."\n\n* If this statem
 ent is not true\, please redo this assignment using your own work before y
 ou submit it.\n\n* If this statement is not included in the submission com
 ment\, the assignment will not be graded.\n\nNote: you must submit your an
 notations (Part 1) on the previous page before your modern adaptation (Par
 t 2) will be graded.\n\nModule Item Completion (browser only)
