BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Historic Halloween Costume (Extra Credit) [IHUM 201-022]
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261102
DTSTAMP:20260903T183000Z
UID:event-assignment-1475750
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:----------------------------------------\nExtra Credit Histori
 c Halloween Costume\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis opti
 onal activity can increase your Activities grade percentage by up to 1%. 
 \n\nInstructions\n------------\n\nDress Up and Take a Photo\n-------------
 ------------\n\nDress up for Halloween in a costume inspired by your histo
 rical and artistic learning in this class and take a photo of yourself. 
 \n\n* Ensure that your costume is BYU-appropriate.\n\n* Must be a photo fr
 om this semester (i.e.\, no middle school or high school Halloween costume
 s).\n\n* Should come from a time period discussed in this class.\n\n* AI g
 enerated images are not allowed.\n\n* Make it fun! Roommates and spouses a
 re welcome to join you!\n\nKeep it simple and use what you already have on
  hand to represent the art\, a person\, or a historical time period time p
 eriod you've studied in this course so far. You do not need to go to a cos
 tume store to come up with a good costume. \n\nWrite Up\n--------\n\nAs w
 ith every writing assignment in this course\, AI is not permitted. All wri
 ting needs to be you from the beginning (including prewriting) to the end.
 \n\nWrite a brief paragraph explaining whom or what you represent—for ex
 ample\, an Egyptian pharaoh\, a Greek goddess\, a Roman philosopher\, or o
 ther historical figure or cultural tradition you've learned about in this 
 course. Include a sentence or two describing the historical significance o
 f your costume.\n\nFor example\, here is Dr. Stanford as the Minoan Snake 
 Goddess\, BYU-Approved version\, along with her write-up.\n\n[snake goddes
 s front.jpg] (https://byu.instructure.com/courses/36390/files/13605137/pre
 view?hidden=1)Example\n---------------------------------------------------
 ---------------------------------------------------------\n\nThis costume 
 is based on the famous Minoan "snake goddess" figurine with her front-and-
 back apron over a very elaborate ruffled skirt. The apron looked quilted t
 o me so I talked my mother\, a quilter\, into making one for me. The origi
 nal figure represents a woman of power\, prominent in Minoan society\, who
  may or may not have been an actual deity. The original figurine is wearin
 g a very low cut vest\, but a modern modest version has the happy effect o
 f switching the focus to the snake\, who is the real star of this costume.
  He's my pet python Balthazar\, and he's non-venomous\, gentle\, and very 
 popular with kids. So with this costume I can promote snake awareness and 
 art history at the same time.\n\nWhat to Submit\n--------------\n\nSubmit 
 both your photo and paragraph as a DOCX or PDF file by 11:59 p.m. two day 
 after Halloween.\n\nModule Item Completion (browser only)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://byu.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _36390&month=11&year=2026#assignment_1475750
