 ing assignment in this course\, AI is not permitted. All writing needs to 
 be you from the beginning (including prewriting) to the end.</p>\n<p>Write
  a brief paragraph explaining whom or what you represent—for example\, a
 n Egyptian pharaoh\, a Greek goddess\, a Roman philosopher\, or other hist
 orical figure or cultural tradition you've learned about in this course. I
 nclude a sentence or two describing the historical significance of your co
 stume.</p>\n<p><span>For example\, here is Dr. Stanford as the Minoan Snak
 e Goddess\, BYU-Approved version\, along with her write-up.</span></p>\n<h
 4 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr cp-txt-dp-primary dp-text-bold" style="m
 argin-top: 50px\; font-size: 18px\; margin-bottom: 10px\; color: #005a38\;
  padding-left: 40px\;">\n<img id="13605130" class="float-end ms-3 mb-3" sr
 c="https://byu.instructure.com/courses/36390/files/13605137/preview?hidden
 =1" alt="snake goddess front.jpg" width="228" height="329" data-api-endpoi
 nt="https://byu.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/36390/files/13605137" data-
 api-returntype="File" loading="lazy">Example</h4>\n<p style="padding-left:
  40px\;">This costume is based on the famous Minoan "snake goddess" figuri
 ne with her front-and-back apron over a very elaborate ruffled skirt. The 
 apron looked quilted to me so I talked my mother\, a quilter\, into making
  one for me. The original figure represents a woman of power\, prominent i
 n Minoan society\, who may or may not have been an actual deity. The origi
 nal figurine is wearing a very low cut vest\, but a modern modest version 
 has the happy effect of switching the focus to the snake\, who is the real
  star of this costume. He's my pet python Balthazar\, and he's non-venomou
 s\, gentle\, and very popular with kids. So with this costume I can promot
 e snake awareness and art history at the same time.</p>\n</div>\n<div clas
